## 2026-10-28 UTC — User-clarified dynamic infrastructure target and version-pin audit

The user confirmed 0.14.1 is the newest available CIGAR and is satisfied with 80–90% infrastructure readiness as long as SDK versions are not hardcoded in application logic. This narrows current acceptance to the reusable core; the extended P0–P10 release/hardening checklist remains unfinished backlog. No newer SDK bytes, publication, installed/platform certification or full crash/load acceptance is invented to close that earlier roadmap.

Audited Rust crates, Python/tooling, dashboard and adapter integration source. All 21 release-literal/constant matches are test-only/gated fixtures. The CIGAR_VERSION 0.14.1 constant/imports are cfg(test); production runtime SDK/core identity comes from resolved catalog or historical legacy metadata, and artifact/handshake checks bind that exact identity. Two focused client modules pass with future/same-version-rebuild/independent-core identities. Existing 65/20 native release/installer and 41 Python generator tests cover generic future routing, exact locks and three deterministic unqualified synthetic drafts. The maintenance runbook now explicitly preserves historical legacy metadata when adding releases.

All six SDK workflows subsequently pass on current image b6486445abe6e50955556388accf6c6de51752106cde9b698bbc5563f0f12322, with 16 intercepted browser cases,148/22/59/3/10 native checks and Rust 1.88/format/ownership/privacy checks recorded in the preceding checkpoint. Broad Clippy/TypeScript retain 36/13 baseline errors in unchanged files, zero new. Earlier masked configuration and workspace/broker/transport observations remain tracked; the successful reruns are not their general root-cause/load remediation proof. A read-only process-name observation was unavailable under the sandbox; no claim of a complete process-loss/descendant cleanup matrix is made.

[Readiness](CIGAR_DYNAMIC_INFRASTRUCTURE.md) records an approximately 85% qualitative engineering estimate for the core target. [Audit receipt](evidence/upgrades-dynamic-scope-20261028.json) binds source classifications, future client checks, exact prior checkpoint, current source inventory and original preservation; all seven approved inputs are rehashed. No runtime source changed after the six passing current-image SDK workflows. Source/lock/qualification data is the compatible-build change boundary; breaking contracts still require a reviewed adapter/Guard update.

The core target requested in the user's clarification is met. Production trust/feed, installed/frozen/other platforms, remaining reset/data-choice/removal/notification/accessibility/crash/approval/load/performance and real newer-release qualification stay explicit release backlog. Original 84 dirty paths retain 75 byte-identical and seven exact inverse-patch-preserved workspace/tutorial files plus the two earlier intentional changes. Original acceptance, locks, Git, live Guard/hooks/preview/system Python/upstream SDK and publication remain preserved.
